From 73d3bfe0c62186f80956b8982bbefcb4ce3ed4b4 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Yuen Su <zypulsecore@gmail.com>
Date: Sun, 18 Oct 2026 14:10:50 +0800
Subject: [PATCH] bpf: accept headers ending at the packet boundary

skb_ptr() rejects a header when its last byte is the last byte of the
packet. A complete UDP or ICMP header with no payload is therefore
unavailable to parse_l4proto(), so its configured DSCP rule is skipped.
One extra payload byte makes the same rule work.

Reject only a pointer past data_end. The full header still has to fit;
truncated headers remain unavailable to the parser.

The complete classifier reproduces this with real kernel BPF test runs
and actual TC ingress/egress on a private TUN interface. The fixed program
passes 976 Ethernet packet cases and 96 L3 datagrams across IPv4/IPv6,
VLAN, ECN, DSCP fallback and truncated-header controls.

Fixes: ca4509cf84d2 ("bpf: switch to using bpf_skb_utils.h")
Signed-off-by: Yuen Su <zypulsecore@gmail.com>
---
 bpf_skb_utils.h | 2 +-
 1 file changed, 1 insertion(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/bpf_skb_utils.h b/bpf_skb_utils.h
index d7378f3..474c992 100644
--- a/bpf_skb_utils.h
+++ b/bpf_skb_utils.h
@@ -33,7 +33,7 @@ skb_ptr(struct __sk_buff *skb, __u32 offset, __u32 len)
 	void *ptr = __skb_data(skb) + offset;
 	void *end = (void *)(long)(skb->data_end);
 
-	if (ptr + len >= end)
+	if (ptr + len > end)
 		return NULL;
 
 	return ptr;
-- 
